Task 2 fix round 1/5. Your schema work is correct — this is a hole in the brief I wrote, plus one claim of yours I checked and could not confirm.

FINDING 1 — CRITICAL, and it is a defect in my brief, not in your work.

`tagsToItemRow` in `lib/ai/parse-tags.ts` maps a validated `Tags` object to the row that gets inserted, and it lists its columns explicitly. It does NOT carry any of the six new fields. So the capture path currently reads: migration adds the columns, the schema captures them from the model, the confirm screen lets the user correct them — and then this function silently drops all six before the insert. Newly captured items would store NULL for every new field while Task 6's backfill populated the OLDER items. Exactly backwards, and it makes the whole migration inert on the path that matters most.

Add to the object `tagsToItemRow` returns, after `seasons`:

```typescript
    accent_color: tags.accent_color,
    branding: tags.branding,
    fit: tags.fit,
    length: tags.length,
    bulk: tags.bulk,
    distressing: tags.distressing,
```

FINDING 2 — the suite is red, and the attribution was wrong.

You reported the two `parse-tags.test.ts` failures as "expected ripple, fixed by Task 3's prompt update". I checked: Task 3 only edits a prompt string in `tag-item.ts`. It cannot touch these fixtures, and nothing else in the plan does either. Left as-is these would stay red through every remaining task.

The actual cause is correct behaviour: `z.enum(...).nullable()` accepts `null` but NOT `undefined`, so the file's `valid` fixture — which omits the six keys entirely — legitimately fails to parse.

⚠️ **Fix the fixture, not the schema.** Add the six keys to `valid` with real values: `accent_color: "sky"`, `branding: "Small"`, `fit: null`, `length: "Ankle"`, `bulk: null`, `distressing: "None"`. Do NOT relax `.nullable()` to `.nullish()` — the model is constrained to return every key, and a genuinely absent one is an error worth surfacing at the boundary.

Then add a regression guard so this cannot silently return:

```typescript
test("tagsToItemRow carries every new styling field into the row", () => {
  const row = tagsToItemRow({
    userId: "u1", imageUrl: "a.jpg", cutoutUrl: null,
    tags: TagSchema.parse(JSON.parse(valid)),
  });
  expect(row.accent_color).toBe("sky");
  expect(row.branding).toBe("Small");
  expect(row.fit).toBeNull();
  expect(row.length).toBe("Ankle");
  expect(row.bulk).toBeNull();
  expect(row.distressing).toBe("None");
});
```

FINDING 3 — you also reported a `tsc` error in `confirm-form.test.tsx` from the same cause. Fix that fixture the same way. It is in scope now: a task does not end with the suite red and the breakage assigned to a later task.

THEN

Run `npm run test:run` — it must be fully green, not "green except known failures" — and `npx tsc --noEmit`. Amend or add a commit as you prefer; both are fine since nothing is pushed. Append a fix report to `.superpowers/sdd/2026-09-02-item-detail-tags/task-2-report.md` with the commands and their output.

Return only: status, commit SHA, the exact final test count, tsc result, and any concerns. Under 10 lines.

Task 3 fix round 2/5. Review came back Approved — this closes two Minor findings that are both inherited from my brief, not defects in your work.

BACKGROUND. The reviewer verified by hand that every enum value named in the prompt matches its schema enum exactly — `BRANDING`, `FITS`, `LENGTHS` (all seven), `BULKS`, `DISTRESSING`. That is correct today. But nothing PINS it: the prompt tests are plain substring checks, so if someone later adds a value to `LENGTHS` and forgets the prompt, or renames one, the model would return a value `TagSchema.parse` rejects and **every tagging call would fail at runtime**. No test would catch it, because the tests assert on the prompt and the schema separately and never against each other.

That is the exact failure mode that has bitten this plan four times already — two things that must agree, changed in separate passes. Worth a real guard.

FINDING 1 — add an enum-fidelity test.

In `lib/ai/__tests__/tag-item-prompt.test.ts`, add a test that imports the enums from `../tagging-schema` and asserts every value appears in `PROMPT`:

```typescript
import { BRANDING, FITS, LENGTHS, BULKS, DISTRESSING } from "../tagging-schema";

test("every enum value the schema accepts is named in the prompt", () => {
  // ⚠️ The prompt and the schema must agree exactly. If a value is added or
  // renamed in one and not the other, the model returns something
  // TagSchema.parse rejects and EVERY tagging call fails at runtime — with no
  // test failing first, because the two were previously asserted separately.
  for (const value of [...BRANDING, ...FITS, ...LENGTHS, ...BULKS, ...DISTRESSING]) {
    expect(PROMPT).toContain(value);
  }
});
```

Before you trust it: confirm it actually FAILS if a value drifts. Temporarily alter one enum value locally, watch the test fail, then revert. Report that you did this — a guard nobody has seen fail is not a guard.

⚠️ One thing to check while writing it: several values appear in more than one enum (`Regular` is in both `FITS` and `BULKS`; `None` is in both `BRANDING` and `DISTRESSING`). That is fine for a `toContain` check, but note it in a comment so a future reader does not think it is a bug.

FINDING 2 — the existing "documents every new field" test omits `distressing`. My brief's list named only five of the six. Add it.

THEN

