Task 6 fix round 2/5. Review came back Approved — error isolation traced sound, no partial-write risk (single atomic UPDATE), and the `fit` rule correctly enforced and non-vacuously tested. Two things to close.

FINDING 1 (Important) — the sentinel depends on model output, and the model has already violated it.

`branding` and `distressing` are both `.nullable()` in `TagSchema`, so the model MAY return null for either. Your own run proves it does: of the 26 filled rows, only **23** have non-null `branding` — three came back null. The scheme survives today only because `distressing` happened to be non-null on all 26. If both ever come back null for one row, that row reads as never-processed and gets silently re-billed. That is the same failure class we just fixed, narrower.

The flaw is relying on model output for a sentinel at all. Make it deterministic — the script writes a value it controls:

```typescript
    // ⚠️ Coerced, not passed through. This column is the sentinel that marks a
    // row as processed, so it must ALWAYS be non-null after a successful run —
    // otherwise the row reads as never-processed and gets re-tagged and
    // re-billed forever. The model is permitted to return null here
    // (`z.enum(DISTRESSING).nullable()`), and in this closet it returned null
    // for `branding` on 3 of 26 rows, so "the model always fills it" is not a
    // property we may rely on.
    // Semantically safe: the prompt already instructs "Use None for a clean
    // garment", so null and "None" carry the same meaning for this field.
    distressing: tags.distressing ?? "None",
```

Leave the other four fields passing through unchanged — `accent_color` null is a genuine, meaningful result, and `branding`/`length`/`bulk` nulls are legitimate too. Only the sentinel column needs the guarantee.

⚠️ Then update the comment above the select predicate: it currently justifies the choice on "both enums carry a literal None", which is true of the vocabulary but was NOT true of the data. Say instead that `distressing` is guaranteed non-null **because this script coerces it on write**.

Add a test pinning it:

```typescript
test("distressing is always written non-null, so the sentinel cannot fail", () => {
  // The model may legitimately return null here; the sentinel may not.
  const written = captureWrite(() => backfillItem({ id: "x", cutout_url: "ok" }));
  expect(written.distressing).not.toBeNull();
});
```
Adapt it to however your existing tests capture the written payload.

FINDING 2 (Minor) — cost logging can undercount. `filled` and its dollar total only increment after a successful `.update()`, so an item whose `tagItem` call succeeded (and was billed) but whose update then failed is counted as `"failed"` with its cost dropped. Track spend at the point the API call succeeds, not at the point the write succeeds, so the total reflects real money.

THEN

`npm run test:run` and `npx tsc --noEmit`. ⚠️ Do NOT re-run the backfill or touch the database — the 26 rows are correct and this change only affects future runs. Append to the report and commit.

